  "problem": "Amy then rolls two fair eight-sided dice, multiplying the numbers obtained. The probability that the product is $12$ or $18$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that the product of the obtained numbers is $12,$ and let $B$ be the event that the product of the obtained numbers is $18.$ Then the probability that the product is $12$ or $18$ is given by $P(A\\cup B).$\n\nSince the product has a single value, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can find $P(A)$ and $P(B)$ by computing the necessary ratios. The product could be anything between $1$ and $64,$ and there are $8\\cdot 8=64$ possible outcomes. \n\nA product of $12$ could be formed in the following $4$ ways:\n\n$$\n{(2,6),(6,2),(3,4),(4,3)}.\n$$\n\nA product of $18$ could be formed in the following $2$ ways:\n\n$$\n{(3,6),(6,3)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{4}{64}, \\\\ P(B) & =\\frac{2}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{64}+\\frac{2}{64}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
